WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.337
Two boar attacks, two very different outcomes.

00:00:03.337 --> 00:00:07.229
Here's how a scene in "House of the Dragon"
had fans thinking back to the first season

00:00:07.229 --> 00:00:08.390
of "Game of Thrones."

00:00:08.878 --> 00:00:12.900
Much of the third episode of "House of the
Dragon" revolves around a rather pathetic

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:17.870
royal stag hunt in the Kingswood, and King
Viserys' troubles regarding matters of family

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:18.907
and succession.

00:00:19.175 --> 00:00:23.790
Princess Rhaenyra Targaryen is technically
traveling with the king, but wants absolutely

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:29.253
nothing to do with the staged hunt, let alone
the clumsy and self-serving social activities around it.

00:00:29.253 --> 00:00:34.370
Making it even less appealing is Lord Jason
Lannister of Casterly Rock, who is aspiring

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:36.143
to win her hand in marriage.

00:00:39.664 --> 00:00:40.792
"Thank you for the wine."

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.289
Rhaenyra chooses to distance herself from
the proceedings by taking a ride in the woods,

00:00:48.289 --> 00:00:52.379
accompanied by her hand-picked Kingsguard
member Ser Criston Cole.

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:56.390
Their stay in the forest ends up going on
quite a bit longer than anyone but Rhaenyra

00:00:56.390 --> 00:01:00.829
is comfortable with, and even includes a brief
encounter with a majestic stag that's markedly

00:01:00.829 --> 00:01:02.769
different from Viserys' experience.

00:01:02.769 --> 00:01:08.020
But it's another notable incident during Rhaenyra
and Ser Criston's forest jaunt brings an entirely

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:10.450
different, and much later, king to mind.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:14.530
As Princess Rhaenyra and Ser Criston are hanging
out in the forest, a mysterious noise from

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:16.850
the bushes prompts them to investigate.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:21.050
The source of the sound turns out to be an
extremely disgruntled wild boar, which charges

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:23.219
Criston and sends the knight flying.

00:01:23.219 --> 00:01:27.000
The boar then attempts to attack and gore
Rhaenyra, but fortunately, the pair manages

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.184
to kill the animal in the nick of time.

00:01:29.184 --> 00:01:33.686
"Game of Thrones" fans will immediately remember
another time a boar attacked a royal in the woods.

00:01:33.686 --> 00:01:38.210
King Robert Baratheon is grievously wounded
when a boar attacks him during a hunting expedition

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:40.280
that also takes place in the Kingswood.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:45.110
This is an incredibly important event, since
Robert's death leads into the arrest and execution

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:50.469
of Ned Stark, and sets the stage for the bloody
succession conflict that fuels the rest of the series.

00:01:50.469 --> 00:01:54.369
While there are obvious similarities between
the boar attacks in "House of the Dragon"

00:01:54.369 --> 00:01:57.720
and "Game of Thrones," there are also very
clear differences.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.579
The boar attack in "Game of Thrones" is a
highly shady affair, as the Lannisters have

00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:04.890
made sure that Robert had plenty to drink
the night before the hunt.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:10.140
The attack itself also happens offscreen between
episodes, as if to underscore the highly underwhelming

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.632
nature of King Robert's rule.

00:02:12.196 --> 00:02:13.249
"My fault.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.505
Too much wine.

00:02:16.299 --> 00:02:17.569
Missed my thrust."

00:02:17.569 --> 00:02:21.569
The boar scene in "House of the Dragon" happens
fully on screen, and the end result of the

00:02:21.569 --> 00:02:24.228
terrifying encounter couldn't be more different,
either.

00:02:24.228 --> 00:02:28.900
While Robert dies a sad, contemplative death
in bed while making a hasty attempt to set

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:33.010
his affairs in order and right past wrongs,
Rhaenyra rides back to the hunting party with

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:37.870
the dead boar in tow, looking more regal than
ever and drawing awed and admiring looks from

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:39.319
everyone who sees her.

00:02:39.319 --> 00:02:43.430
The effect of Rhaenyra bagging a dangerous
boar with just one knight is further enhanced

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:47.340
by the fact that many of the people present
just saw her father's bungling attempts to

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.400
put a stag out of its misery, despite the
fact that people were literally holding it

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.819
for him to stab.

00:02:52.819 --> 00:02:57.180
So when Robert meets a boar, his rule — and
his life — come to a pitiful end.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.239
Rhaenyra encounters one and not only lives
to tell the tale, but gains bucketloads of

00:03:01.239 --> 00:03:04.830
respect that'll surely prove worth its weight
in gold going forward.

